Being absurd is useful precisely because it can break the tyranny of the expected frame without requiring you to destroy the frame.

Absurdity is not just randomness. Good absurdity preserves enough structure that the mind can still recognize the game, then violates one or more assumptions in a way that exposes them:

\text{expected rule}
\rightarrow
\text{deliberate violation}
\rightarrow
\text{surprise}
\rightarrow
\text{new relation becomes visible}

That makes absurdity a cousin of humor, surrealism, satire, improvisation, and some forms of scientific thought experiment.

It can do several jobs at once: reveal hidden assumptions, lower social defensiveness, create memorable compression, open otherwise forbidden conceptual territory, and generate candidate hypotheses that ordinary reasoning would never search.

But the useful version needs a return path. If everything becomes absurd, you lose contrast and therefore lose the instrument.

So I’d give it a Pantheon form:

\boxed{\text{Absurdity Operator}}

Hold one invariant fixed. Violate another assumption aggressively. Observe what survives. Return with the useful delta.

For example:

What if gravity repelled instead of attracted?

Physically absurd in our ordinary world, but suddenly you have to notice how much architecture, locomotion, atmosphere, biology, and language quietly presuppose gravity behaving as it does.

Or socially:

What if everyone had to announce the incentive behind every sentence before saying it?

Absurd—but it exposes how much communication relies on implicit motive.

So absurdity becomes a probe.

And your “for our own gain” works best when the gain is learning, creativity, resilience, or better coordination rather than merely gaining leverage over somebody else. Otherwise absurdity can turn into confusion-as-control, which is much less interesting.

The strongest form is almost:

\text{absurdity} =
\text{permission to temporarily disobey the obvious}

while keeping enough Golden Thread to find your way home.

That’s probably why fools, jesters, tricksters, clowns, surrealists, and comedians keep appearing across cultures. They can say or enact something that would sound intolerable in ordinary register, and because the frame is “play,” everyone gets to inspect it without immediately having to defend themselves.

A jester is sometimes just a scout wearing bells.